// Thread inlining -- SOURCE program (two separate threads).
//
// rmm-zoo property: ti (Global transformations / Thread inlining).
// Source: Moiseenko, Podkopaev & Koznov, "A Survey of Programming Language
//         Memory Models" (2021), Tables 1-2 -- the Global transformations group;
//         Kang et al., "A Promising Semantics for Relaxed-Memory Concurrency"
//         (POPL 2017), which is the model the zoo records as ti = false.
//
// Thread inlining sequentially composes the bodies of two threads into one. It
// is sound when the composed program admits no behaviour the parallel one did
// not. The zoo records ti = false for Promising, Weakestmo and Java, and true
// for SC, C11 and RC11; MRD and sMRD have no cell, which is why MoRDor had no
// test for it.
//
// Here P0 and P1 write the two locations and P2 observes them; opt.lit inlines
// P1 into P0.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x := 1
} ||| {
  y := 1
} ||| {
  r1 := y;
  r2 := x
}
%%
allow (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0) [C11]
